  "theorem_name": "RadixSelectorFullRepresentationHistogramAndPrecisionDepthNoGo",
  "declared_proposition": "For a block of B Goldbach counts A_i, set b=B+1 and let h_a be the number of targets with A_i=a. If U=max A_i, then the TICKET-215 selector E=sum b^(-A_i) satisfies b^U E=sum_(a=0)^U h_a b^(U-a). Because 0<=h_a<b, the base-b digits of this integer recover the entire representation-count histogram exactly, including the exception count h_0. This is exact encoding, not an arithmetic estimate of unseen counts.",
  "mathematical_argument": "Grouping equal exponents gives E=sum h_a b^(-a). Multiplication by b^U produces an integer whose base-b digit at place U-a is h_a. No carry occurs because h_a<=B=b-1, and uniqueness of finite radix expansions proves exact recovery. The pairs [1,M] and [1,M+1] have different histograms but selector distance (b-1)/b^(M+1), so uniform fixed absolute precision cannot recover arbitrarily deep histogram digits.",
